        (15, "Examine urban alienation and the weakening of community bonds as social consequences of rapid Indian urbanisation.", "Anomie describes a real social-psychological consequence of density and anonymity, but it operates alongside continuing village linkage and cannot be extended into a general claim about urban disorder without evidence.", [8, 9, 15, 2]),
        (20, "Assess the relationship between Tier-2 city growth, the new middle class and the culture of consumption.", "Tier-2 growth produces differentiated work, housing and consumption at the same time, so an assessment that reads organised retail as shared prosperity misses the informal worker and the informal settlement inside the same city.", [18, 17, 10, 16]),
        (20, "Evaluate the claim that urban investment becomes socially progressive only when tenure, services and voice follow the networks that attract people.", "The networks that attract migrants also outrun formal housing and services, so investment becomes progressive only where tenure, connection and procedural voice are extended to the excluded rather than where visible infrastructure is concentrated.", [1, 4, 7, 11, 14, 17]),
